## 2. UNIT TESTING

### 2.1 Unit Test Standards

**Coverage Requirements:**
```
Target Coverage: 80% overall
Critical Paths: 95% minimum
- Authentication/Authorization
- Trade execution
- Tax calculations
- Compliance rules

Excluded from coverage:
- Trivial getters/setters
- Generated code
- Configuration classes
- Third-party integrations
```

**Example Unit Test - Java:**
```java
@DisplayName("Portfolio Return Calculation")
class PortfolioReturnCalculatorTest {
    
    private PortfolioReturnCalculator calculator;
    private Portfolio portfolio;
    
    @BeforeEach
    void setup() {
        calculator = new PortfolioReturnCalculator();
        portfolio = new Portfolio();
    }
    
    @Test
    @DisplayName("Should calculate TWR correctly with single cash flow")
    void testTWRWithSingleCashFlow() {
        // Arrange
        portfolio.setBeginningValue(100_000);
        portfolio.addCashFlow(new CashFlow(50_000, 0.5));  // Day 15 of 30
        portfolio.setEndingValue(160_000);
        
        // Act
        double twr = calculator.calculateTWR(portfolio);
        
        // Assert
        assertEquals(0.0476, twr, 0.0001);  // 4.76%
    }
    
    @Test
    @DisplayName("Should throw exception for negative values")
    void testNegativeValueValidation() {
        portfolio.setBeginningValue(-100_000);
        
        assertThrows(IllegalArgumentException.class, 
            () -> calculator.calculateTWR(portfolio));
    }
    
    @Test
    @DisplayName("Should handle zero beginning value")
    void testZeroBeginningValue() {
        portfolio.setBeginningValue(0);
        portfolio.addCashFlow(new CashFlow(50_000, 0.5));
        portfolio.setEndingValue(50_000);
        
        double twr = calculator.calculateTWR(portfolio);
        
        // Result depends on business logic
        assertTrue(Double.isFinite(twr));
    }
}
```

